WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.279
Molar mass connects a sample's mass with its amount of substance.

2
00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:10.357
Three symbols will guide the calculation. Have paper and a
calculator ready. Write

3
00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:15.380
the quantity and unit for small m, capital M and n. Pause now.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.404
A question mark is useful if you are unsure; check it when you

5
00:00:20.404 --> 00:00:20.790
resume.

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.955
Small m is the sample's mass in grams. Capital M is molar mass,

7
00:00:26.955 --> 00:00:32.110
in grams per mole: the mass of one mole. n is the amount

8
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.472
of substance in moles. Correct your own labels before moving on.

9
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:42.407
Ten grams of magnesium oxide is our sample. One mole has a mass

10
00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:47.281
of forty grams. Should the sample contain less than one mole or
more

11
00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:52.155
than one mole? Pause and write your prediction with a reason
before doing

12
00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:52.530
arithmetic.

13
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:58.108
The sample is one quarter of the mass of one mole. Its amount

14
00:00:58.108 --> 00:01:02.683
is therefore one quarter of a mole. These bars compare masses of
the

15
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:07.259
same substance. Keep that prediction in mind as we choose and use
the

16
00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:07.610
equation.

17
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:14.843
Magnesium oxide has one magnesium for every oxygen in its
formula. Using the

18
00:01:14.843 --> 00:01:21.018
provided atomic masses, twenty-four plus sixteen gives forty
grams per mole. Count the

19
00:01:21.018 --> 00:01:22.919
formula subscripts before adding.

20
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:29.376
To find the amount, divide sample mass by molar mass. Ten divided
by

21
00:01:29.376 --> 00:01:34.819
forty gives zero point two five moles. It agrees with our
prediction. Keep

22
00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:40.261
units with given quantities and the final answer; substitute
numbers in the working

23
00:01:40.261 --> 00:01:40.680
line.

24
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:47.029
The starting equation is supplied. Complete the substitution and
answer for eighteen grams.

25
00:01:47.029 --> 00:01:52.325
Pause and write now. If you need help, identify which number is
sample

26
00:01:52.325 --> 00:01:57.620
mass and which is the mass of one mole. Try the step before

27
00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.250
continuing to the solution.

28
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:05.490
Eighteen divided by forty gives zero point four five moles. If
you reversed

29
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.714
the fraction or multiplied, return to the question: how much of
one mole's

30
00:02:10.714 --> 00:02:15.938
forty-gram mass do we have? Correct the line yourself. A hint
helps you

31
00:02:15.938 --> 00:02:19.153
learn; the next fresh attempt needs less support.

32
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:26.047
Now the unknown is mass. Starting from n equals m divided by M,

33
00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:31.894
multiply both sides by M. That gives mass equals amount
multiplied by molar

34
00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:37.291
mass. Choose the equation from the quantity requested, rather
than automatically dividing.

35
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:44.698
Zero point three zero moles is the amount. First predict the
mass, compared

36
00:02:44.698 --> 00:02:51.062
with forty grams, then write the calculation. Pause now. Explain
why multiplication makes

37
00:02:51.062 --> 00:02:52.531
sense this time.

38
00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:59.438
Amount multiplied by molar mass gives twelve grams. Three tenths
of a mole

39
00:02:59.438 --> 00:03:05.343
has three tenths of forty grams. The result is below forty grams,
as

40
00:03:05.343 --> 00:03:11.248
predicted. Round final numerical answers to two decimal places
unless told otherwise; keep

41
00:03:11.248 --> 00:03:13.519
full calculator values between steps.

42
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:19.995
Carbon dioxide changes the formula. Close the previous working
and solve this on

43
00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:25.457
paper. Count the atoms represented in the formula, then find
molar mass and

44
00:03:25.457 --> 00:03:30.919
choose your equation. Pause before continuing. This attempt
should show what you can

45
00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:32.600
do without a hint.

46
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.082
The two oxygens make the molar mass forty-four grams per mole.
Twenty-two grams

47
00:03:39.082 --> 00:03:44.563
is half that mass, so the amount is zero point five zero moles.

48
00:03:44.563 --> 00:03:50.045
If you used twenty-eight for molar mass, you missed the oxygen
subscript. Correct

49
00:03:50.045 --> 00:03:51.732
the formula count first.

50
00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:57.893
Close the model and explain your equation choice from memory.
Correct one error,

51
00:03:57.893 --> 00:04:03.054
then try fresh studio practice. When your teacher assigns a
Canvas checkpoint, submit

52
00:04:03.054 --> 00:04:08.214
it independently for marking and review. Return after a gap and
check whether

53
00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:11.389
you can still choose and use the method.
